Received in revised form: 
10 December 1998 
Accepted: 23 December 1998
Key words Stiff-man syndrome · 
Type 1 diabetes · GAD65 
autoantibodies · Plasmapheresis · 
Immunosuppression
Sirs: Stiff-man syndrome (SMS) is a
rare neurological disorder character-
ized by involuntary fluctuating stiff-
ness and rigidity of axial skeletal
muscles and superimposed painful
muscle spasms which are often in-
duced by startle or emotional stimuli
[1–3]. This disorder is thought to re-
sult from an immune-mediated defi-
ciency of the inhibitory neurotrans-
mitter g-aminobuturic acid (GABA).
Autoantibodies to glutamic acid de-
carboxylase (GAD), the enzyme re-
sponsible for the synthesis of GABA,
are present in the serum and in the
cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) of 50–60%
of patients with a clinical diagnosis
of SMS [4–6]. There are two major
isoforms of GAD, GAD65 and
GAD67, which are encoded by two
separate genes and share approxi-
mately 65% amino acid sequence
[7–8]. Patients with SMS have au-
toantibodies directed against both
GAD isoforms but react preferen-
tially with GAD65. Among SMS pa-
tients approximately one-third are
also affected by other autoimmune
diseases, primarily type 1 diabetes
[4, 5, 9].
Type 1 diabetes is the result of a
specific immune-mediated destruc-
tion of beta cells in the pancreatic
islets. It has been demonstrated that
autoantibodies to GAD65 are present
in the sera of approximately 70–80%
of new onset type 1 diabetes patients
and often appear long before the on-
set of clinical symptoms [10, 11]. Al-
though GAD autoantibodies are
found in both SMS and type 1 dia-
betes, the GAD autoantibody titers
and epitope specificities differ
markedly between the two diseases.
GAD65 autoantibodies from SMS
patients recognize a combination of
linear and conformational epitopes,
while GAD65 autoantibodies in pa-
tients with type 1 diabetes sera are
predominantly directed against the
conformational epitopes [12–14].
An agonist of GABA transmis-
sion such as diazepam or baclofen is
the current treatment for patients
with SMS [1–3]. Although GABA
agonists are effective in ameliorating
disease-associated stiffness and
rigidity by reducing motor neuron
firing, they do not attenuate the pro-
gression of the disease. A variety of
immunotherapeutic approaches have
been tested. Response to plasma-
pheresis has been inconsistent with
both a beneficial response and no ef-
fect being reported [15–19]. Im-
provement following steroid treat-
ment or intravenous immune globu-
lin administration has also been re-
ported in several cases [20–24]. We
report a patient with both SMS and
type 1 diabetes who did not fully re-
spond to the conventional treatment
with diazepam and baclofen. Several
immunotherapeutic approaches in-
cluding plasmapheresis, prednisone,
intravenous immune globulin (IVIG),
cyclophosphamide, mycophenolate
mofetil (MMF) were subsequently
administrated over a 2-year period,
and GAD65 autoantibody levels
were monitored throughout treat-
ment.
<seg id="s0001" group="l0001" targets="p1" role="patient_specific">The patient is a 36-year-old white
woman with a 5-year history of type
1 diabetes (diagnosed in 1992) and
hypothyroidism due to Hashimoto’s
thyroiditis (antimicrosomal antibodies
were positive at 1 : 40). After devel-
oping diabetes she experienced re-
peated episodes of ketoacidosis and</seg>
<seg id="s0002" group="l0001" targets="p1" role="patient_specific">developed severe insulin resistance.
Insulin resistance was ameliorated
with insulin lispro (Humalog) via an
insulin pump; however, the mecha-
nism of insulin resistance and re-
sponse to insulin lispro, was not de-
termined. Approximately 2 years after
the diagnosis of diabetes (during the
summer of 1994) she started to de-
velop intermittent pain and stiffness
in her abdominal musculature. She
had an extensive negative workup for
her abdominal pain including liver
function studies, fecal fat studies, an
abdominal ultrasound, abdominal
computed tomography, and an endo-
scopic retrograde cholangiopancrea-
tography. By the autumn of 1994 she
had developed lower extremity weak-
ness with intermittent pain. Results of
the neurological examination was un-
remarkable except for decreased mus-
cle strength (3–4/5) in her left lower
extremity. Magnetic resonance imag-
ing of the brain and cervical spine
was normal. A sural nerve biopsy re-
vealed mild axonopathy, and a muscle
biopsy specimen showed mild dener-
vation and reinnervation. At the end
of 1995, in addition to her progres-
sive abdominal pain and lower ex-
tremity weakness, she developed in-
tense pain in her upper and lower ex-
tremities. The severity of the pain ne-
cessitated hospitalization for intra-
venous narcotics, which were ineffec-
tive. She also complained of rigidity
and spasms of her proximal muscles
making ambulation difficult. SMS
was diagnosed in 1996 by finding
high titers (> 1 : 1000) of GAD65 
autoantibodies in both the serum and
the CSF. The di
agnosis of SMS was
confirmed clinically by the improve-
ment in muscular pain and stiffness
with oral diazepam. An EMG per-
